  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 547 AM EDT TUE OCT 27 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Wednesday evening with a low-amplitude\npattern across the CONUS - broad 500 hPa ridging in place over the\nEastern CONUS with upstream troughing over the Western CONUS. At the\nsurface, ~1030 mb surface high pressure is centered off the New \nEngland Coast with ridging extending southwestward across the \nSouthern Appalachians to the western Gulf Coast. To our northwest,\nlow pressure is over the Upper Midwest with a cold front \nextending southwest into Nebraska and then northwest across the \nNorthern Rockies to another weak low near Vancouver Island.\n\nThe trough will be amplifying over the Western CONUS at the start\nof the period, which will be accompanied by corresponding, albeit\nweak, height rises over much of the eastern CONUS into Friday. \nThis being the case, surface high pressure off the Atlantic shears\napart, with the westward portion cresting over the Southern \nAppalachians on Thursday night. Ridge-valley temperature splits \nare likely to sharpen for both Wednesday and Thursday nights with\nthe loss of cloud cover allowing for more efficient radiative\ncooling. This should also favor decent nocturnal fog formation in\nthe prone valleys, mainly near lakes, rivers, and larger creeks. \n\nA northern-stream shortwave trough then rides over the ridge on \nFriday. This will support the cold front sagging southward toward\nthe Ohio River, stalling over or just north of our area due to a \nloss of upper-level support. With this setup, the next notable \nweathermaker (upper-level energy associated with former tropical \nsystem Vance in the eastern Pacific) will rise northward across \nMexico and attempt to ride through and quash the lingering ridge.\nThis robust disturbance will send PWATs surging higher to between\n1.2 and 1.3 inches by Sunday, exceeding the 90th percentile of \nLREF climatology for this time of year. The combination of upper- \nlevel energy and Gulf moisture surging northward and interacting \nwith the stalled boundary will set the stage for our first \nrainfall in about 2 weeks across most of eastern Kentucky. Present\nmodel timing favors this disturbance's associated rainfall \ndeveloping late Saturday night. With that being said, the forecast\nstill bears watching on Halloween evening, as a slightly faster \narrival could mean the difference between a completely dry and \nmild evening versus an increasingly damp one.\n\nIn sensible terms, look for mostly clear skies to return Wednesday\nevening and persist through Thursday night before thickening clouds\nreturn for Friday and especially the upcoming weekend. Rain chances\narrive late Saturday and Saturday night, peaking on Sunday (around\n60 percent PoP) before diminishing, at least temporarily, for\nMonday. High temperatures in the mid 70s can be expected through\nSaturday, then cool slightly for Sunday and Monday with thicker\ncloud cover and higher rain chances. Nighttime lows will generally\nbe coolest on the first couple of nights, ranging in the 40s to\nnear 50F, but then moderating into the 50s over the weekend.",
